.TH "TDDA LS" 1 "May 2026" "3.0" "tdda ls manual"
.SH NAME
\fBtdda ls\fR — List fields in a dataset

.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBtdda ls\fR [\fB-h\fR] [\fB-1\fR|\fB--one-line\fR] [\fB-l\fR] [\fB--pandas\fR] [\fB--polars\fR]
         [\fB--backend\fR \fIBACKEND\fP]
         \fIINPUT\fP

.SH POSITIONAL ARGUMENTS
\fIINPUT\fP is one of:
  - a CSV file (or \fB\&.tsv\fR, \fB\&.psv\fR, \fB\&.txt\fR)
  - a Parquet file (\fB\&.parquet\fR)
  - a flat file with colon syntax to trigger metadata lookup
    (e.g. \fBfoo.csv:\fR)
  - a flat file with an explicit metadata path
    (e.g. \fBfoo.csv:foo.serial\fR)

.SH DESCRIPTION
The \fBtdda ls\fR command lists the fields in a dataset.

.PP
Without \fB--long\fR, it prints a one-line summary followed by the field
names, right-aligned.

.PP
With \fB--long\fR, it prints a one-line summary followed by a table showing
each field's dtype, minimum value, maximum value, and null count.

.PP
For flat files, a second line reports how the file was read and which
metadata file was used, if any.

.SH OPTIONS
\fB-h\fR, \fB-?\fR, \fB--help\fR        Show this help message and exit

.PP
\fB-1\fR, \fB--one-line\fR          List all field names on one line, space-separated
.br
\fB-l\fR, \fB--long\fR              Show dtype, min, max, and null count per field

.PP
\fB--pandas\fR, \fB--pd\fR          Use Pandas as DataFrame engine (default)
.br
\fB--polars\fR, \fB--pl\fR          Use Polars as DataFrame engine
.br
\fB--backend\fR, \fB-B\fR \fIBACKEND\fP   Backend choice for Pandas
.br
                          \fBn\fR for numpy_nullable *
.br
                          \fBa\fR for pyarrow
.br
                          \fBo\fR for original

.SH EXAMPLES
The example data can be obtained by running \fBtdda examples\fR, which will
create various directories, including \fBserial_examples\fR\&.

.PP
1) \fBtdda ls accounts1k.parquet\fR

.PP
List the fields in \fBaccounts1k.parquet\fR\&.

.PP
2) \fBtdda ls -l accounts1k.csv:\fR

.PP
Show field details for \fBaccounts1k.csv\fR, using any associated metadata
file found automatically.

.PP
3) \fBtdda ls -l accounts1k.csv --polars\fR

.PP
Show field details using Polars.

.SH SEE ALSO
